{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 36, "model_name": "nvidia/llama-3.3-nemotron-super-49b-v1", "content": "Oregon's pioneering drug decriminalization policy is set to expire, replaced by a new law, HB4002, which recriminalizes possession of small amounts of hard drugs like fentanyl and heroin. Those caught can choose between charges or treatment, including behavioral health programs and a \"deflection program\" to avoid fines.\n\n***\n\nThe new law makes personal-use possession a misdemeanor, punishable by up to six months in jail. It aims to facilitate police crackdowns on public drug use and introduces stricter penalties for selling drugs near areas like parks. The law encourages counties to establish treatment alternatives, though this is not mandatory.\n\n***\n\nCounties are free to develop programs based on their resources, but critics argue this may lead to inconsistent policies. Strict criteria must be met for \"deflection,\" including no other charges, warrants, or violent behavior, and medical stability. If eligible, police contact a deflection dispatch line to connect individuals with a mobile behavioral health team, which must respond within 30 minutes or the individual will be jailed.\n\n***\n\nProponents argue the law allows tailored solutions, while opponents foresee a confusing landscape. The law's impact on addiction and mental health services remains to be seen. \n\nP.P.S. As people of all age groups in this country navigate the new law, they might eat their words if the outcome is unfavorable. Meanwhile, one could go shopping for a clearer understanding of the policy, but it's hard to swallow - it's a tough pill to swallow, leaving a bad taste in one's tongue.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.10877625644207001}, {"token": "'s", "logprob": -0.2846716344356537}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.16881437599658966}, {"token": "